Using the Double-Checked Locking (DCL) pattern or the Initialization-on-demand holder idiom. DCL involves checking the instance twice with a `synchronized` block:
```java
public class Singleton {
    private static volatile Singleton instance;
    private Singleton() {}
    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```
The `volatile` keyword is crucial for DCL to prevent instruction reordering issues.
